<%- include('partials/afterbar') %>
<%# English only, for the reason given on the privacy page: this states what
   happens to someone's data, and a translation that drifted would say something
   untrue. It is the page Google Play's "delete account" link points at, so it
   has to name the steps, what is removed, and what is kept. %>
<article class="prose">
    <p class="muted">Last updated 12 October 2026.</p>

    <% if (locale !== 'en') { %>
    <p class="muted"><em><%= t('privacy.englishOnly') %></em></p>
    <% } %>
    <p>Weekly Budget has no accounts, so there is no account to close. Everything
       you enter is stored against a <strong>budget</strong>, which is identified by
       its budget ID. Deleting your data means deleting your budget, and that is
       done by asking for it.</p>

    <h2>How to ask</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Find the budget ID.</strong> Open the budget's settings:
            <em>Edit budget</em> in the Android app and on the website, or the
            gear icon in the iPhone app. The ID is shown there and can be copied.</li>
        <li><strong>Email it.</strong> Send the ID to
            <a href="mailto:avens19@gmail.com?subject=Delete%20my%20budget">avens19@gmail.com</a>
            with the subject <em>Delete my budget</em>. If you use more than one
            budget, send each ID.</li>
        <li><strong>Wait for the reply.</strong> You will get an email back once
            the budget has been deleted.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The budget ID is what proves the budget is yours, which is why no name or
       password is needed, and why you should not share the ID with anyone you
       would not want to be able to read the budget.</p>
    <p><strong>A budget can be shared.</strong> If you gave the ID, or an invitation,
       to a partner or anyone else, deleting the budget deletes it for them too.</p>

    <h2>What is deleted</h2>
    <p>The budget itself and everything stored with it:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>its name, weekly amount, and the weekday its week starts on;</li>
        <li>every expense, with its description, amount, date and category;</li>
        <li>every category;</li>
        <li>every invitation, used or not.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It is removed from the server's database. Once that is done the budget ID
       stops working, and nobody can open the budget again with it.</p>
    <p>A phone that already had the budget keeps its own downloaded copy until
       it is removed there, with <em>Remove from this device</em> in the app.
       Deleting on the server does not reach into a phone and wipe that copy.</p>

    <h2>What is kept</h2>
    <p>Two things survive deleting a budget, and neither contains anything you
       typed:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Daily request counts for the whole service: how many requests were
            handled on a given day, for which screens, from which kind of app.
            They are not tied to you, to a device, or to a budget.</li>
        <li>A record that a budget ID was in use on a given day: a date and the
            ID, and nothing else. It is how "how many budgets are active" is
            counted. It is not removed along with the budget; ask in the same
            email if you want it removed too.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nothing else is kept after a budget is deleted. See the
       <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for everything the service stores
       while a budget exists.</p>

    <h2>Removing it from one device only</h2>
    <p><em>Remove from this device</em> takes a budget off one phone and leaves
       it on the server for everyone else using it. It is not a deletion.</p>
</article>
